app sha256-spec [
  version: "1.0.0",
  description: "Design contract for SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) over a bytes input, written in .lolo over the strict 32-bit bit/* operators (bit/xor, bit/and, bit/or, bit/not, bit/shr, bit/rotr); every sum of words is wrapped with (math/mod ... 4294967296) before the next bit operation. SHA256_DIGEST { data, encoding } answers SHA256_DIGESTED { digest } where digest is the 32-byte hash as lowercase hex (64 chars) when encoding is hex, or standard padded base64 (44 chars) when encoding is base64, and SHA256_REFUSED { reason } otherwise, never a partial digest. Reasons are explicit and closed: encoding (not hex or base64), data (input is not bytes). Padding, the 64-word message schedule and the 64 rounds are the std atom's own; the spec fixes only names and types. Golden vectors computed with python3 hashlib: empty input = e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855; abc (3 bytes) = ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad; the 448-bit NIST message abcdbcdecdefdefgefghfghighijhijkijkljklmklmnlmnomnopnopq (56 bytes, forces a second padding block) = 248d6a61d20638b8e5c026930c3e6039a33ce45964ff2167f6ecedd419db06c1; one million bytes of 0x61 (1,000,000 bytes, 15625 blocks, too large to embed so the test generates it with bytes/repeat or an equivalent loop) = cdc76e5c9914fb9281a1c7e284d73e67f1809a48a497200e046d39ccc7112cd0; a 1 KB binary blob whose byte i is (i*7+3) mod 256 for i in 0..1023, base64  = e9183d9a79aad8a047b8e67981210d50b01fc75b1edba5bc32ba3d3ec4d5056d, and with encoding base64 the digest is 6Rg9mnmq2KBHuOZ5gSENULAfx1se26W8Mro9PsTVBW0=. Registry tie-in: the exact bytes of a 112-byte gzip tarball (USTAR with one entry package/a.txt holding hello and a newline, mtime 0, gzip level 9 mtime 0) H4sIAAAAAAAC/+3NQQrCQBAEwDnnFb4g2WDMfxYJCgYMZgWf7+pJvCuIVZceug+z5P0pH6Yut+VW4jNSNQ7DM6v3fKwvd+37tO13sUnxBde15Et9H//pOM3zuQkAAAAAAAAAAAB+zB0brt2nACgAAA== hash to 7c7bb8966ae91e839200fbd074cd9ae0af7419f690f7808cd3cf92e813b4bfda, the value a package-registry integrity check compares after the gunzip step reads the same bytes. Boundary lengths to test: 55, 56, 63, 64 and 65 bytes (padding spills into a second block at 56). A word operand outside 0..4294967295 reaching a bit operator is a defect of the atom, not a refusal reason. Values it replaces: the host-side SHA-256 call of the package install integrity check. Padding boundaries (hex): 55 × \"a\" → 9f4390f8d30c2dd92ec9f095b65e2b9ae9b0a925a5258e241c9f1e910f734318; 56 × \"a\" → b35439a4ac6f0948b6d6f9e3c6af0f5f590ce20f1bde7090ef7970686ec6738a; 63 × \"a\" → 7d3e74a05d7db15bce4ad9ec0658ea98e3f06eeecf16b4c6fff2da457ddc2f34; 64 × \"a\" → ffe054fe7ae0cb6dc65c3af9b61d5209f439851db43d0ba5997337df154668eb; 65 × \"a\" → 635361c48bb9eab14198e76ea8ab7f1a41685d6ad62aa9146d301d4f17eb0ae0."
]

orbital Sha256Spec {
  expects entity Sha256Run [runtime] {
    id : string!
    digest : string
  }
  expects trait Sha256 {
    state idle {
      SHA256_DIGEST -> idle
    }
    emits {
      SHA256_DIGESTED { requestId : string  digest : string! }
      SHA256_REFUSED { requestId : string  reason : string! }
    }
  }
  expects page "/sha256" -> Sha256
}
